class:: PitchMPM
summary:: McLeod Pitch Method with NSDF clarity
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Pitch
related:: Guides/MIRa, Classes/Pitch, Classes/Pitch2, Classes/Tartini, Classes/PitchYIN

description::
Implements the McLeod Pitch Method from McLeod and Wyvill (2005). The normalized square difference function (NSDF) is derived from FFT autocorrelation and prefix energies; local maxima above code::cutoff:: times the strongest peak are considered, and the earliest qualifying peak is refined parabolically. Clarity is the selected NSDF peak height from 0 through 1, not a calibrated probability.

The analyzer uses a trailing causal frame and updates every code::hop * frameSize:: samples. Approximate first-result latency is one frame. The effective minimum frequency cannot fall below code::2 * sampleRate / frameSize::. Silence and failed voicing return three zeros.

It is practically similar to sc3-plugins link::Classes/Tartini::, which is historically MPM-based, and core link::Classes/Pitch::. MIRa is an independent paper-based implementation with explicit frame, hop, silence, and clarity semantics rather than behavioral or numerical compatibility.

classmethods::

method:: kr

argument:: in
Audio-rate signal. Multichannel expansion creates independent analyzers.

argument:: minFreq
Requested minimum F0 in Hz.

argument:: maxFreq
Maximum F0 in Hz, below Nyquist.

argument:: frameSize
Literal power-of-two frame size from 256 through 8192.

argument:: hop
Update hop as a frame fraction from 0.0625 through 1.

argument:: cutoff
Fraction from 0.5 through 1 of the strongest NSDF peak used for peak selection.

returns::
An Array: frequency in Hz, NSDF clarity from 0 through 1, and voiced flag 0 or 1.

method:: latency
Returns approximate first-result latency in seconds.

instancemethods::

examples::
PitchMPM estimates fundamental frequency and NSDF clarity using the McLeod Pitch Method. In this example, it drives an analog sub-bass synthesizer with dynamic wavefolding: sustained notes synthesize a rich octave-below bassline, with clarity modulating wavefolding warmth. An interactive code::MIRaGUI.pianoRoll:: tracks the fundamental pitch.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIRaGUI.soundPath("melody.wav");
    ~miraBuffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~miraBus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~miraExample = {
        var source = if(~miraBuffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~miraBuffer, BufRateScale.kr(~miraBuffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic melody fallback
            Saw.ar(Select.kr(LFNoise0.kr(2).range(0, 5), [220, 261.63, 293.66, 329.63, 392, 440])) * 0.2
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var mpm = PitchMPM.kr(source, 80, 1200, 2048, 0.25, 0.93);
        var freq = mpm[0];
        var clarity = mpm[1];
        var subFreq = (freq * 0.5).clip(30, 600);
        var subOsc = Saw.ar(subFreq.lag(0.03)) * clarity.lag(0.04);
        var folded = (subOsc * 1.5).fold2(0.8) * 0.3;
        var sound = (source * 0.25) + folded;

        if(~miraBus.notNil) { Out.kr(~miraBus, freq) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) ! 2
    }.play;

    ~miraGUI = MIRaGUI.pianoRoll("MPM Pitch Tracker", ~miraBus, 36, 84);
});
)
::
code::
(
~miraExample.free;
if(~miraGUI.notNil) { ~miraGUI.close };
if(~miraBus.notNil) { ~miraBus.free };
if(~miraBuffer.notNil) { ~miraBuffer.free };
)
::
